                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\ast^s
            %p
          %h3#Examples3Manifolds 3-Manifolds
          %p The following is straightforward:
          #SpinManifoldAdmitsFraming.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-manifold"} 3-manifold
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              admits a framing.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              That a 3-manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              means that we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+of+the+structure+group"} reduction of the structure group
              of the tangent bundle to the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mi Spin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to B Spin(3)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(3)
              has vanishing
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
              below degree 3. Therefore its
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B Spin(3)
              has vanishing homotopy groups below degree 4, and hence every morphism out of a 3-dimensional manifold into it is homotopically constant.
          %p But in fact, the following stronger statement is also true.
          #EveryOrientable3ManifoldsIsParallelizable.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-manifold"} 3-manifold
              admits a framing.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              By the argument in the proof of prop.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinManifoldAdmitsFraming"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+Stiefel-Whitney+class"} second Stiefel-Whitney class
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi w
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_2
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wu+class"} Wu class
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wu+class"} Wu class
              vanishes. This, in turn, is by definition defined to represent the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steenrod+square"} Steenrod square
              under
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
          #In4dFirstOrderGravity.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EveryOrientable3ManifoldsIsParallelizable"}
              has some impact in the context of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
              fields on 4-dimensional
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime+manifolds"} spacetime manifolds
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              (time) with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-manifold"} 3-manifold
              (space). Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EveryOrientable3ManifoldsIsParallelizable"}
              implies that already when that spatial 3-manifold is orientable, then the whole globally hyperbolic spacetime admits a framing. (See also at
              = succeed ".)" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/teleparallel+gravity"} teleparallel gravity
            %p
              In fact, for general
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+spacetimes"} Lorentzian spacetimes
              of dimension 1+3 it remains true that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              exists if the total 4D
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
              admits a framing (
              %a{:href => "spin+structure#Geroch68"}> Geroch 1968
              , see
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "spin+structure#OverLorentzianManifolds"} there
          %h3#spheres Spheres
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
              that admit a framing are precisely only
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the 0-sphere
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 0
                          %mo =
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^0 = \ast \coprod \ast
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              %li
                %p
                  the 1-sphere
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
                  underlying the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
                  (the unit
                  = succeed ");" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
              %li
                %p
                  the 3-sphere
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 3
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} special unitary group
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi SU
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
              %li
                %p
                  the 7-sphere
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 7
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^7
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moufang+loop"} Moufang loop
                  internal to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
            %p
              where the algebras appearing are precisely the four
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebras"} normed division algebras
          %p
            This is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Adams58"} Adams 1958
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+spectral+sequence"} Adams spectral sequence
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#IntersectionPairing Relation to intersection pairing and Kervaire invariant
          %p
            On a framed manifold, there is a canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+refinement"} quadratic refinement
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+pairing"} intersection pairing
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kervaire+invariant"} Kervaire invariant
          %h2#properties_2 Properties
          %h3#moduli_of_framings Moduli of framings
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+framed+manifolds"} moduli space of framings
            on a fixed manifold is a disjoint union of subgroups of the oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+class+group"} mapping class group
            which fix a given isotopy type of framings.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+framing"}> normal framing
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+twisted+framing"} normal twisted framing
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+link"}> framed link
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+braid"} framed braid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+elliptic+curve"} framed elliptic curve
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+framed+manifolds"} moduli space of framed manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/teleparallel+gravity"} teleparallel gravity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-framing"} 2-framing
          %p
            Formalization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
            is discussed
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/differential+cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos#GLnTangentBundles"} there
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            Review in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kervaire+invariant"} Kervaire invariant
            includes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Akhil+Mathew"}> Akhil Mathew
              ,
              %em The Kervaire invariant I
              (
              %a{:href => "https://amathew.wordpress.com/2012/10/01/the-kervaire-invariant-i/#more-3888"}> web
              )
          %p The theorem about the parallizablitiy of spheres is due to
          %ul
            %li#Adams58
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Adams"}> John Adams
              ,
              %em On the Non-Existence of Elements of Hopf Invariant One
              Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 64, 279-282, 1958, Ann. Math. 72, 20-104, 1960.
          %p
            Relation to existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connections"} flat connections
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Thorpe65
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Thorpe"}> John Thorpe
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Parallelizablility and flat manifolds
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/ThorpeParallelizable.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %h3#for_2manifolds For 2-manifolds
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+framed+surfaces"} moduli space of framed surfaces
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oscar+Randal-Williams"}> Oscar Randal-Williams
              ,
              %em Homology of the moduli spaces and mapping class groups of framed, r-Spin and Pin surfaces
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.5366"}> arXiv:1001.5366
              )
          %h3#for_3manifolds For 3-manifolds
          %p
            For
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-manifolds"} 3-manifolds
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rob+Kirby"}> Rob Kirby
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Melvin"} Paul Melvin
              = succeed "," do
                %em Canonical framings for 3-manifolds
              %strong 23
              1 (1999) &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/KirbyMelvon3Framings.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The relation to “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-framings"}> 2-framings
            ”:
          %ul
            %li#Atiyah
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
              ,
              %em On framings of 3-manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/atiyahfr.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h3#left_invariant_framings_on_compact_connected_lie_groups Left invariant framings on compact connected Lie groups
          %ul
            %li#Ossa82
              %p
                Erich Ossa, Lie groups as framed manifolds, Topology, 21 (1982), 315–323, (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-9383%2882%2990013-1"}> doi
                )
            %li#Minami16
              %p
                Haruo Minami,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On framed simple Lie groups
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jmsj/1468956166"} pdf
          %p
            For an extension to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-compact+groups"} p-compact groups
            see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tilman+Bauer"}> Tilman Bauer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em p-compact groups as framed manifolds
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://people.kth.se/~tilmanb/publication/p-compact-groups-as-framed-manifolds/"} paper
        .revisedby
